{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786481691,"nonce":"2JPxuY5p+3G487lFSibuFb4MkGM=","title":"**What is wrong.** Three adapters in `internal/app/adapters.go` perform no translation. One is an identity function, and two are nil-guard-plus-forward wrappers whose guards are either dead or duplicated by the value they wrap. The composition root as a whole is legitimate — nearly every other type in the file does real work at a boundary depguard forbids either side crossing — which is exactly why these three read as adapters when they are not.","message":"**The demonstration.** `internal/app/adapters.go:899` is the whole of `snapshotSource`:\n\n```go\nfunc snapshotSource(repo *notifrepo.SnapshotRepository) notifservice.SnapshotSource { return repo }\n```\n\nIt converts a concrete to an interface it already satisfies, and that fact is already proved at compile time three files away: `internal/app/assertions.go:118` reads `_ notifservice.SnapshotSource = (*notifrepo.SnapshotRepository)(nil)`. The single call site is `internal/app/container.go:679`. Its doc-comment at `:893-898` argues the function marks a documented swap point — *\"once `alerts/service` publishes an equivalent, the wiring swaps one constructor\"* — but the swap point is the assignment at `container.go:679`, which is one line either way, and the assertion is what would actually catch the drift.\n\n`lateNotificationReader` (`:800-831`) is a settable box for late binding: `notification` is constructed after `alerts`, so `container.go:438` creates `\u0026lateNotificationReader{}` and `container.go:520` fills `inner` once `buildNotification` has run. Its three methods each guard `l == nil` and return zero values. That branch is unreachable — `container.go:438` always stores a non-nil pointer and nothing ever replaces it. Worse, the guard checks the wrong nil: the value that is genuinely absent during construction is `l.inner.svc`, and the guard does not test it. It does not need to, because the inner `notificationReader` already guards it on every method (`:164-166` and its two siblings), which is the guard doing the real work. Strip the outer type and `*notificationReader` is a settable box with the same behaviour and one nil-check per method instead of two.\n\n`groupVersions` (`:789-798`) is the closer call and this ticket does not claim it is empty. Its guard is `g == nil || g.svc == nil`, and the second half is live: `container.go:437` creates it empty and `container.go:484` fills `svc`, so there is a real construction window in which version `0` is returned, which `:787-788` documents as *\"the documented degraded answer: the notify worker resolves the real version at evaluation time.\"* Only the `g == nil` half is dead, for the same reason as above.\n\n**Why it matters.** A named type in the composition root is a claim that something happens at that boundary, and a reader of `adapters.go` has to open each one to find out that three of them do nothing. `snapshotSource` is the sharper case: it duplicates a compile-time assertion with a runtime function, so a reader who edits it learns nothing the assertion would not have told them, and a reader who trusts it believes the swap point is enforced somewhere it is not. The dead `l == nil` and `g == nil` branches are worse than noise — they suggest a nil is possible here, which is why the one nil that is possible got guarded in the wrong place.\n\n**Where.** `internal/app/adapters.go:893-899` (`snapshotSource`), `:800-831` (`lateNotificationReader` and its three dead guards), `:789-798` (`groupVersions`, dead outer half only), `:157-166` (`notificationReader`, which already carries the live guard), `internal/app/assertions.go:118,56,57`, `internal/app/container.go:437-438,484,520,679`.\n\n**Scope.** small\n\n**Done when.** `container.go:679` passes `notifrepo.NewSnapshotRepository(general, clk)` directly and `snapshotSource` is deleted, leaving `assertions.go:118` as the only drift check. The dead `l == nil` and `g == nil` branches are gone, and `lateNotificationReader` is either removed in favour of a `*notificationReader` box or keeps only the guard that is reachable.","files":null}]}